INFERENCE WEEKLY
Friday, March 20, 2026

Hello. The week in running models, for people who pay the bill.

COBALT-4: early-access notes
Meridian AI's next model is in early access with about 200 customers ahead of a launch reported for Monday. What we can say from the readers under NDA who replied in generalities: tool use is noticeably better, latency is similar to the current generation, and there is a new pricing tier for non-interactive traffic that sits between standard and batch. We will cover the pricing in detail once it is public.

CACHE HIT RATES, MEASURED
We asked readers to report prompt-cache hit rates from their provider dashboards. Forty replies. Support and extraction workloads with a fixed system prompt: 60-85% hit rate. Document-heavy workloads with varying context: 5-20%. Agent loops: 30-50%, because the conversation prefix stays stable while the tail grows. The pricing pages show the discount; the hit rate is what you actually get.

A SERVING FRAMEWORK BREAKS ITS API
The most widely used open-source inference server shipped a major release this week with a changed configuration schema and a new default scheduler. Teams that upgraded without reading the notes report throughput regressions of up to 30% until they re-tuned. The old defaults are available behind a flag. Pin your versions.

FIVE LINKS
1. The migration guide for the serving framework release, which you should read before upgrading.
2. A well-argued post on treating model choice as a procurement decision.
3. A dataset of real prompt-cache hit rates by workload type (ours, from the replies above).
4. A hardware teardown of the new inference ASIC.
5. The best explanation of 4-bit quantization for people who do not want the math.

ONE NUMBER: 30%. The throughput regression from upgrading the serving framework without re-tuning. Read the notes.

That's the week. Reply with what you are running.

